WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.913
Water sovereignty.

00:00:01.863 --> 00:00:03.693
The tenth dimension.

00:00:03.693 --> 00:00:12.340
Twenty-eight or more long-term drinking water advisories persist on First Nations reserves despite eight point two billion dollars allocated to address the crisis.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.647
Clean water is a basic human right recognised by the United Nations.

00:00:16.647 --> 00:00:21.034
Canada voted in favour of the UN resolution on the human right to water.

00:00:21.034 --> 00:00:27.704
Yet Canada fails to provide clean drinking water to thousands of its own citizens — primarily Indigenous communities.

00:00:27.704 --> 00:00:31.613
The government promised to end all long-term water advisories.

00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:33.557
Billions were spent.

00:00:33.557 --> 00:00:35.443
Twenty-eight remain.

00:00:35.443 --> 00:00:39.784
Some communities have been under boil water advisories for over a decade.

00:00:39.784 --> 00:00:46.602
A country that cannot provide clean water to its own people on its own territory has failed the most basic test of sovereignty.

00:00:46.602 --> 00:00:49.931
Water sovereignty is the tenth dimension of failure.

00:00:49.931 --> 00:00:52.931
Three dimensions of water sovereignty failure.
